  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nA reinforcing cool front is passing this morning, with winds\nreturning to the northeast at 10-12 mph for today. This air \nmass is forecast to persist through Monday, with high \ntemperatures near seasonal averages in the mid to upper 70s. \nWinds are predicted to return from the south on Monday; and \nthis will be a transition day to warmer weather. The only \nchanges within the Tuesday to Saturday time frame will be the \nstrength of the persistent south winds. Given a weak pressure \ngradient, winds will only average 10-15 mph during the day and 5\nto 10 mph or less at night. Winds will increase from the south \nat 15-25 mph by Saturday as the mid to upper level flow becomes \nlight southwesterly, encouraging surface troughing across the \ncentral and northern high plains. The various ensemble means \nindicate 850mb temperatures steady at about 20C at Dodge City \nfrom Tuesday through Saturday, suggesting at least mid 80s for \nhighs, with upper 80s certainly possible in a few spots. \nHowever, given a little more in the way of downslope flow by \nSunday, highs in the 85 to 90 degree range look more likely, \nwith 90th percentile 850mb temperatures around 22-23C. But this \nis assuming that we don't have a cold frontal passage before \nSunday afternoon. The ensemble means suggest a cooldown by \nMonday or Tuesday; but some of the ensemble members show the \nfront passing Sunday. Bottom line is that there is a high degree\nof certainty that warm and dry weather will persist through \nSaturday; but there is uncertainty thereafter.",
